/-- Two directed Bregman half-square lower bounds imply strong monotonicity of
the selected gradient.

For a carrier Bregman formula with evaluation map `eval`, if the two opposite
directions each dominate one half of the squared evaluated distance, their sum
is exactly the gradient-difference pairing and controls the full squared
distance.

Layer: Layer0 | Gap: Level 0 (Bregman coercivity to gradient monotonicity)
Proof: add the two pointwise Bregman lower bounds, rewrite the swapped norm,
  unfold the carrier Bregman formula, and normalize the inner-product algebra.
Source: Mathlib real inner-product algebra and SOptLib carrier Bregman formula APIs
Used in: accelerated primal-dual dual prox stability from DGF Bregman coercivity
Book citation: book/FOML/StochasticAcceleratedPrimalDual.json#/assumptions/3
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for
  Machine Learning, stochastic accelerated primal-dual method -/
theorem gradient_strongMonotone_of_bregman_half_sq_lower_bound
    {P E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    (v : P → ℝ) (eval grad : P → E) (y z : P)
    (hyz :
      (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖eval y - eval z‖ ^ 2 ≤
        carrierBregmanFormula v eval grad z y)
    (hzy :
      (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖eval z - eval y‖ ^ 2 ≤
        carrierBregmanFormula v eval grad y z) :
    ‖eval y - eval z‖ ^ 2 ≤
      ⟪eval y - eval z, grad y - grad z⟫_ℝ := by
  have hsum := add_le_add hyz hzy
  have hlhs :
      (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖eval y - eval z‖ ^ 2 +
          (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖eval z - eval y‖ ^ 2 =
        ‖eval y - eval z‖ ^ 2 := by
    rw [norm_sub_rev (eval z) (eval y)]
    ring
  have hrhs :
      carrierBregmanFormula v eval grad z y +
          carrierBregmanFormula v eval grad y z =
        ⟪eval y - eval z, grad y - grad z⟫_ℝ := by
    unfold carrierBregmanFormula
    simp only [inner_sub_right, inner_sub_left, real_inner_comm]
    ring
  rwa [hlhs, hrhs] at hsum


